404 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H6
/P 329 0 R
/T (The model describes how schools can design and sustain career-connected learning by aligning instructional experiences, organizational structures, and external partnerships around a common set of principles. It operates alongside traditional high school coursework rather than functioning as a stand-alone program. Students continue to take core academic classes such as English, mathematics, science, and social studies at their home high schools while participating in pathway-based learning that connects those subjects to real industries and applications.)
/E (The model describes how schools can design and sustain career-connected learning by aligning instructional experiences, organizational structures, and external partnerships around a common set of principles. It operates alongside traditional high school coursework rather than functioning as a stand-alone program. Students continue to take core academic classes such as English, mathematics, science, and social studies at their home high schools while participating in pathway-based learning that connects those subjects to real industries and applications.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
405 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H6
/P 329 0 R
/T (The model serves students in grades 11 and 12, the years when learners prepare to transition to college, careers, or other postsecondary pathways. Students apply to the program and must be on track toward graduation to participate. This requirement ensures they have the academic foundation to engage in advanced project-based learning, industry-aligned coursework, and work-based learning. Once accepted, students split their school day: part at their home school for core academics, part at the STEAM Center for pathway instruction, industry-informed coursework, and work with professionals in their selected field. This shared schedule keeps students connected to their home school community while giving them access to a specialized career-connected learning environment. )
/E (The model serves students in grades 11 and 12, the years when learners prepare to transition to college, careers, or other postsecondary pathways. Students apply to the program and must be on track toward graduation to participate. This requirement ensures they have the academic foundation to engage in advanced project-based learning, industry-aligned coursework, and work-based learning. Once accepted, students split their school day: part at their home school for core academics, part at the STEAM Center for pathway instruction, industry-informed coursework, and work with professionals in their selected field. This shared schedule keeps students connected to their home school community while giving them access to a specialized career-connected learning environment. )
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
